insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('32b79aa6-b1ec-585c-ac90-c70bc121520d', 'rg10-argument-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Bring Back the Bay Ferry$q$, $q$Every weekday morning, hundreds of drivers in Port Ellery inch across the Callow Bay Bridge, turning a three-mile trip into a forty-minute crawl. The town council should restart passenger ferry service across the bay, and it should do so by next summer.

A ferry once served this route. From the 1920s until 1961, when the bridge opened, the Callow Bay ferry carried about six hundred riders a day. Those numbers show that a ferry is a natural fit for our town.

Residents want this service today, too. Last month I stood at the Harbor Street bus stop, which sits right beside the old ferry dock, and asked people waiting there whether they would ride a ferry. Of the 85 people who answered, 70 said yes.

Other towns have shown that the idea works. Across the state, Wrenfield launched a small ferry four years ago, and it now carries more than four hundred passengers on a typical weekday. Wrenfield's bridge charges a toll of nine dollars, while ours is free, but a good idea is a good idea.

A ferry would also eliminate traffic on the Callow Bay Bridge entirely. Once commuters discover how relaxing it is to cross the water with a cup of coffee, nobody will want to drive.

Some council members have raised concerns about the cost of docks and boats. Frankly, the people who oppose the ferry simply do not care about the future of our waterfront. Anyone who loves Port Ellery should support this plan.

The bay has always been the heart of our town. It is time to use it again.$q$, 'draft')
