-- Passage rg10-argument-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d05da80b-fa2c-5556-8bd0-41925b11b7bc', 'rg10-argument-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Did the Red Pigment Travel?$q$, $q$At the Sallow Ridge rock shelter, archaeologists have found dozens of ancient handprints and animal figures painted in deep red ochre, a pigment made from iron-rich earth. Dr. Teodora Vasquez, who leads the excavation, argues that the pigment did not come from nearby. She believes it was carried roughly 300 kilometers from a quarry at Kettle Hill, which would make it evidence of long-distance trade between distant communities.

Her argument rests on chemistry. Every ochre deposit contains small amounts of other elements, such as manganese and titanium, in proportions that vary from place to place. These proportions act like a fingerprint. Vasquez's team tested four paint samples from the shelter walls and found that their fingerprints closely matched ochre from Kettle Hill and did not match the two deposits closest to the shelter.

Other researchers find the match intriguing but not yet convincing. Geologist Arjun Malhotra points out that the region around Sallow Ridge has never been fully surveyed. Smaller ochre beds, some possibly with a chemistry similar to Kettle Hill's, could lie within a day's walk of the shelter. If so, the painters might have gathered their pigment locally. Malhotra also notes that four samples are a small number for a site with more than forty painted figures, some of which may have been made centuries apart.

Vasquez agrees that more work is needed. Her team plans to test twenty additional samples this year, and a separate group will map ochre deposits within fifty kilometers of the shelter.

For now, the chemical match is genuine and relevant evidence. Whether it is enough to prove a trade route is a different question, and the answer may depend on what the new surveys uncover.$q$, 'draft')
